# Copy this file to .env and fill in real values before running
# `docker compose up`. Nothing here has a default baked into the image
# or docker-compose.yml itself - every one of these is required
# (docker-compose fails fast with a clear "set this in .env" message if
# left unset), matching this whole project's own established
# fail-closed posture (real TLS/JWT_SECRET requirements outside Docker
# work the same way).

# The real Postgres superuser password for the postgres container -
# used once, by the migrate service, to bootstrap the real
# assetlogic_app application role (NOSUPERUSER NOBYPASSRLS - see
# db/bootstrap.sql's own comment for why that matters).
POSTGRES_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD=

# The real password for the assetlogic_app role api/gateway actually
# connect as - NEVER the superuser password above, by design.
ASSETLOGIC_APP_PASSWORD=

# 32+ random bytes - e.g. `openssl rand -hex 32` (or, on Windows, in
# PowerShell if openssl isn't installed - a real compatibility bug
# found live: `[System.Convert]::ToHexString` needs modern .NET, not
# available in plain Windows PowerShell 5.1's .NET Framework runtime):
# $b=New-Object byte[] 32; (New-Object Security.Cryptography.RNGCryptoServiceProvider).GetBytes($b); -join($b|%{$_.ToString("x2")})
JWT_SECRET=

# 32 bytes of hex, e.g. `openssl rand -hex 32` - optional. Leaving this
# unset disables WMI-based remote deployment and managed local-admin
# accounts (a clean 503 from their own endpoints), everything else
# still works.
SECRET_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Where THIS deployment is actually reachable from the outside - real
# agents and the console both need this to be correct, not localhost,
# for anything beyond testing on the same machine `docker compose up`
# ran on.
PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8080
REMOTE_DESKTOP_WS_BASE_URL=ws://localhost:9091
# Which browser origin the API accepts real requests from - must be
# changed alongside the two lines above (same real host, same real
# port the console itself is served on, :8081 by default in this
# compose stack). Left at the localhost default, a real deployment's
# signup/login fails with a browser-blocked CORS error that shows in
# the console as a generic "is the API running?" - a real, previously
# missed gap, not a hypothetical one.
CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGIN=http://localhost:8081

# Real, REQUIRED for remote desktop to actually work through this Docker
# packaging - the SAME real server address as the two lines above. Without
# this, the browser<->gateway WebRTC connection tries to use the gateway
# CONTAINER's own internal Docker network address (never reachable from a
# real browser outside this host), and every session fails ~15s after
# consent with "timed out establishing the real-time connection" even
# though everything up to that point looks completely healthy. A real,
# previously-missed gap found live on an actual pilot client's Docker
# deployment - not needed at all for the direct/bare-metal (non-Docker)
# gateway.exe setup, since there's no container NAT in the way there.
GATEWAY_PUBLIC_IP=

# Real video-codec remote desktop (see services/gateway/videoencode.go)
# needs a real ffmpeg binary on the GATEWAY host only - never on managed
# endpoints. The Docker Compose gateway image already bundles it
# (Dockerfile.gateway); FFMPEG_PATH only needs setting for a non-
# standard install location, e.g. this project's own dev-machine setup
# (see README.md). Leaving it entirely unset/unresolvable is a real,
# graceful fallback to the original JPEG-over-DataChannel relay, not a
# hard requirement.
# FFMPEG_PATH=

# Real P2P (agent<->browser direct) remote desktop transport (see
# services/gateway/turnserver.go and proto/agent/v1/agent.proto's own
# "Real P2P" header comment). TURN_PUBLIC_IP is this gateway host's own
# real, routable address - there's no reliable way for the process to
# discover this itself, so it's required to opt in at all. TURN_PORT
# defaults to 3478 (the standard STUN/TURN port) if unset.
# TURN_SHARED_SECRET mints a real, short-lived, per-session credential
# for every attempt (the standard TURN REST API scheme) - required
# alongside TURN_PUBLIC_IP; this refuses to run an unauthenticated open
# relay if only one of the two is set. Leaving both unset is a real,
# graceful fallback to the classic, fully gateway-relayed path, not a
# hard requirement.
# TURN_PUBLIC_IP=
# TURN_PORT=3478
# TURN_SHARED_SECRET=

# Real remote-desktop session recording (see
# services/gateway/sessionrecording.go's own header comment for the
# full design/scope). Set this to a real, writable directory on the
# GATEWAY host to record every gateway-relayed session as a real .webm
# file (RECORD_SESSIONS_DIR/<session_id>.webm) - reuses the exact same
# ffmpeg dependency as the video-codec feature above, so a host with no
# ffmpeg on PATH simply never records, no separate config needed.
# services/api ALSO needs this SAME value set (and SAME filesystem -
# a real, named, single-host limitation for now, see README) to serve
# recordings back to the console. Leaving it unset is a real, graceful
# no-op - zero behavior change, matching every other opt-in feature
# here. Real, honest scope: never covers a P2P session (see the video
# codec's own P2P mutual-exclusivity note above - the gateway never
# sees a P2P session's media at all).
# RECORD_SESSIONS_DIR=

# onprem (default) or cloud - see the README's licensing section for
# what actually differs between the two.
DEPLOYMENT_MODE=onprem

# Real one-click "Apply Update" (see services/api/systemupdate.go and
# the watchtower service in docker-compose.yml) - a real, random secret
# shared between the api and watchtower containers so api's own
# authenticated internal call is the only thing that can ever trigger
# an update. Generate the same way as JWT_SECRET above, e.g.
# `openssl rand -hex 32`. Leaving this unset is a real, supported state:
# the "Apply Update" button's own endpoint reports itself honestly
# unconfigured (503) instead of erroring, and updates still work exactly
# as before - a manual `docker compose pull && docker compose up -d`
# on this host.
WATCHTOWER_API_TOKEN=

# Which registry to pull the api/gateway/console/backup images from -
# only matters if you're pulling pre-built images rather than building
# from source (`docker compose build`). Defaults to this product's own
# real published images; override only if you're running your own
# private mirror/registry.
# IMAGE_REGISTRY=ghcr.io/logicalthiker
# IMAGE_TAG=latest

# A real signed license string from cmd/license-issuer - optional.
# Leaving this unset (and LICENSE_FILE_HOST_PATH below) runs core
# module only, a real supported state, not an error.
LICENSE_KEY=
# LICENSE_FILE_HOST_PATH=./path/to/a/real/license.txt

# Real Razorpay billing (pkg/billing, services/api/billing.go) -
# optional. Leaving both unset makes every /api/v1/billing/* endpoint
# correctly report itself unconfigured rather than erroring - most
# on-prem/self-hosted deployments never need this at all. Get a real
# test-mode Key Id/Key Secret from Settings > API Keys in the Razorpay
# dashboard (Test Mode) - see README.md's own billing writeup for why
# Razorpay rather than Stripe, and what's actually built (checkout per
# billing period, not yet a silent auto-recurring mandate).
RAZORPAY_KEY_ID=
RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET=

# Real, self-hosted AI backend for the ai-copilot/autonomous-remediation
# modules (both separately licensed) - optional, off by default like
# every other integration in this file. Two things both have to happen
# to turn this on, deliberately never one implying the other:
#   1. Run with the "ai" Compose profile enabled - either
#      `docker compose --profile ai up -d`, or set
#      COMPOSE_PROFILES=ai in this .env file, so the bundled `ollama`
#      service (an OpenAI-wire-compatible server, zero code changes
#      needed) actually starts and pulls its real model.
#   2. Set OPENAI_API_KEY below to any non-empty value (Ollama itself
#      doesn't check it - "ollama" is a fine placeholder). This is the
#      actual on/off switch api.exe reads; OPENAI_BASE_URL/OPENAI_MODEL
#      already default correctly to the bundled Ollama container below,
#      so leaving THIS empty is what keeps AI genuinely off even if the
#      "ai" profile happens to be enabled for some other reason.
# To point at a real cloud AI vendor INSTEAD of the bundled Ollama, set
# OPENAI_BASE_URL/OPENAI_MODEL/OPENAI_API_KEY to that provider's own real
# values here and skip the "ai" Compose profile entirely - services/api's
# own client is a plain OpenAI-wire-compatible HTTP client, it doesn't
# care which server answers.
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=http://ollama:11434/v1
# OPENAI_MODEL=llama3.2:3b
OPENAI_API_KEY=
# CPU-only inference is genuinely slower than a cloud API (a real ~47s
# per completion was measured live against this exact default model) -
# raise further for a larger model or a slower host; lower back toward
# 30 if pointed at a real, fast cloud vendor instead.
# OPENAI_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=120

# Real TLS directly on the api container's own listener - optional, off
# by default (api runs plain HTTP unless BOTH are set). Most deployments
# don't need this: a real reverse proxy (nginx/Traefik/Caddy) in front
# of this whole compose stack, or a cloud load balancer, is the more
# common place to terminate TLS. This exists for reaching the api
# container directly with nothing in front - see docker-compose.yml's
# own commented-out block (mirrors the gateway service's identical
# pattern) for how to mount a real cert/key pair.
# TLS_CERT_FILE=
# TLS_KEY_FILE=

# Real, opt-in offsite copy of the scheduled `backup` service's own
# dumps (pkg/releasestore, cmd/upload-backup) - optional, unset by
# default. Leaving BACKUP_OFFSITE_BACKEND unset keeps every backup
# exactly as local-disk-only as it always has been; set it to "s3" or
# "azblob" plus that backend's own vars below to additionally push each
# real dump to a real bucket/container, closing
# compliance/business-continuity-plan.md's own named "no offsite/
# durable copy" gap. See README.md's own writeup for how this was
# live-verified.
# BACKUP_OFFSITE_BACKEND=s3
# BACKUP_OFFSITE_S3_BUCKET=
# BACKUP_OFFSITE_S3_ENDPOINT=
# BACKUP_OFFSITE_AZURE_CONTAINER=
# BACKUP_OFFSITE_AZURE_CONNECTION_STRING=
# BACKUP_OFFSITE_AZURE_ACCOUNT_URL=
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# AWS_REGION=us-east-1

# Real hardware-change email notifications (pkg/mailer, gateway's own
# notifyHardwareChange) - optional, unset by default. Leaving SMTP_HOST
# unset means every Owner/Admin in your Organization still sees a real
# hardware-change alert in the console's Inventory Management page (the
# device_hardware_changes row is always recorded regardless), just with
# no email sent - a real, honestly-degrading state, not an error. Works
# against any real SMTP relay: your own Exchange/Office365 relay, Gmail
# SMTP, or SendGrid/Mailgun's own SMTP endpoints. SMTP_PORT defaults to
# 587 (STARTTLS) if unset; set it to 465 for a relay that requires
# implicit TLS from the first byte instead. SMTP_FROM defaults to
# SMTP_USER if unset.
# SMTP_HOST=
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_USER=
# SMTP_PASSWORD=
# SMTP_FROM=
